import
	../../html

intro-js html[]()
	- section "Building JavaScript",
		- p quote
			To build to JavaScript, run a command like {code "keen build foo.keen --out foo.js"}.
			{br}
			Then include it in a web page using {code "<script type=\"module\" src=\"/foo.js\"></script>"} .
		- p quote
			You can also run {code "node foo.js"} .
			Node.js support is mostly intended for quick testing and not as the main build target.

		- aside-small
			- p quote
				Despite using a {code "module"} target,
				Keen currently doesn't support defining a module with imports and exports.
				It only supports building to a script that executes the {function "main"} function on load.
			- p quote
				If you want to call Keen code from JS code, you could have the {function "main"}
				function generate an object full of functions and store it to a global variable.

	- section "Calling JavaScript functions",
		- p quote
			Now we'll write "Hello, world" directly using JavaScript APIs.
		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/js

			main void() js, global, unsafe
				alert js-any = js-global["alert"]
				_ = call alert, "Hello, world!"

		- p quote
			The {js-any} type can hold any JavaScript value.
			Basically any function using this type is {unsafe}.
		- p quote
			{fn-js-global} is equivalent to {code "window"} (or {code "self"}) in the browser
			or {code "global"} in Node.js .
		- p quote
			{fn-js-subscript} reads a property from a JS object.
			So {function "js-global"}{lbracket}{string "\"alert\""}{rbracket} is equivalent to
			{code "window.alert"} in JS.
		- p quote
			{fn-call} is a JS function call.
			So {fn-call}{sp}{variable "alert"}{code ", "}{string "\"Hello, world!\""}
			is equivalent to {code "alert(\"Hello, world!\")"} in JS.
		- p quote
			{fn-call} returns {js-any}.
			{code "_ ="} drops the {js-any} value to get {type "void"}.

	- section "Use JavaScript objects",
		- p "To call a method on a JavaScript object, use {fn-call-property}."
		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/js
				keen/js/dom

			main void() js, global, unsafe
				home = call-property document, "querySelector", "#test-p"
				home["style"]["border"] := "1px solid pink"

		- p (id: "test-p"), quote
			This paragraph has ID {code "test-p"}. After running the above script it will have a border.


		- p quote
			The first line above is equivalent to {code "document.querySelector(\"#test-p\")"} in JS.
			{br}
			The second line is equivalent to {code "home.style.border = \"1px solid pink\";"} in JS.

		- aside-small quote
			As with Java, Keen {string}s are not JS {string}s.
			{br}
			A string literal can directly create a {js-any} value.

	- section "Conversion",
		- p quote
			There is no guarantee about how Keen values are represented in JS.
			{br}
			For example, a Keen {string} is not a JS {code "string"}.
		- p "{std-module "keen/js"} defines many {function "to"} functions for converting between Keen and JS values."
		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/js

			main void() js
				n nat = 1
				j js-any = n.to
				log j.to::nat

	- section "Make JavaScript objects",
		- p quote
			{std-function "keen/js/util", "named-new"} from {std-module "keen/js/util"}
			is useful for creating JS {code "object"}s.
		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/js
				keen/js/util

			main void() js, unsafe
				o js-any = x: 1, y: 2
				log o["x"].to::nat

	- section "Async",
		- p "To call a JavaScript {code "async"} function, simply call and {std-function "keen/js", "await"} it."
		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/js

			main void() js, global, unsafe
				log fetch-text "/hello.txt"

			fetch-text string(url string) js, global, unsafe
				fetch = js-global["fetch"]
				response = await call fetch, url.to
				to::string await call-property response, "text"

		- p quote
			For best performance, try to avoid doing async things in a deeply nested functions,
			since this makes their callers (and callers' callers, etc.) async functions too.

		- aside-small quote
			{std-module "keen/js/browser"} has convenient typed {std-function "keen/js/browser", "fetch"} functions.

	- section "Callbacks",
		- p "A Keen lambda can be converted to a JS function using {function "to"}."
		- p "These functions support lambdas taking {void} or {js-any} and returning {void} or {js-any}."
		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/js

			main void() js, global
				sleep 5000
				log "done"

			sleep void(msec float) global, js
				do trusted
				resolver = of resolve js-any
					:: void
					_ = call js-global["setTimeout"], resolve, msec.to
				_ = await call-new js-global["Promise"], resolver.to

	- aside-small "{std-module "keen/io/time"} has a more convenient {std-function "keen/io/time", "sleep"} function."

	- section "Imports",
		- p quote
			Keen does not yet have the ability to import JavaScript modules directly.
			{br}
			There is a {std-function "keen/js", "js-import"} function though.
		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/js
				keen/js/util

			main void() js, global, unsafe
				module = js-import
					"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/canvas-confetti@1.9.3/+esm"
				# This calls the default export
				_ = call-property module, "default",
					particleCount: 100
					spread: 100

	- section "Other JavaScript operations",
		- p quote
			If Keen doesn't have a function for doing what you want with JS,
			you can write a JS snippet using {function "eval"}.
		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/js

			main void() js, unsafe
				log loose-equal 1, "1"

			loose-equal bool(a js-any, b js-any) js, unsafe
				f = eval quote
					(x, y) => x == y
				to::bool call f, a, b

-fn-call html-node()
	std-function "keen/js", "call"
-fn-call-property html-node()
	std-function "keen/js", "call-property"
-fn-js-global html-node()
	std-function "keen/js", "js-global"
-fn-js-subscript html-node()
	std-function "keen/js", "subscript"
